1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Welcome back. Every capable agent rests on two patterns, and we'll walk through both.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
The first is the tool-use loop, where the model acts and then reacts to what it sees.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
The second is sub-agents, which keep focused work from crowding your main context. Let's look at the details.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Three ideas anchor everything that follows. First, the tool-use loop. Without tools, a model can only produce

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
text. Give it tools, and it can act. It requests a tool, the runtime runs that tool, and the result comes back,

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
so the model can decide what to do next.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Second, the augmented model. That's the real building block underneath every agent, a model wired up with retrieval,

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
tools, and memory, so it does more than generate.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Third, the sub-agent. That's a separate agent your main agent spawns to handle one focused piece of work.

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Running in its own space and reporting back just its answer. Keep these three straight, and the rest

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,000
of this lesson clicks into place.

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Let's slow down on the loop, because it's the engine of every agent. On each pass,

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
the model looks at the task and decides whether it needs a tool. If it does, it names the tool and

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:16,000
the input it wants.

15
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Your runtime executes that call, maybe reading a file or hitting an API, and hands the result back.

16
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Now the model has a fresh observation, and it decides again. Maybe it calls another tool.

17
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Maybe it has enough to answer. This repeats turn after turn until the model responds with plain text and no tool call.

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
That text-only reply is the signal that the loop is done. Nothing magical, just request, execute, observe, repeat.

19
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Here's a detail that trips people up. Not every tool call runs the same way.

20
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Read-only tools, the ones that just observe, like reading a file, searching, or running a query,

21
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
have no side effects.

22
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Because they can't step on each other, the runtime is free to run several of them at once,

23
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
concurrently, which saves you real wall clock time. State modifying tools are different.

24
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Anything that writes a file, edits code, or runs a shell command changes the world.

25
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
So the runtime runs those one at a time, in order to avoid conflicts.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
One thing to watch with your own custom tools,

27
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,000
they default to sequential. If a custom tool only reads, mark it read-only, so it can join the parallel group.

28
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Now to the second pattern, the sub-agent, and the most important reason to use one.

29
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Context isolation. When you delegate a subtask to a sub-agent, it runs in its own separate conversation.

30
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
All the noisy middle steps, the exploratory tool calls, the dead ends, the long tool outputs,

31
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:55,000
they stay inside that sub-agent's context. Your main agent never sees them.

32
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,000
What comes back to the parent is just the sub-agent's final message, a clean summary of the result.

33
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
So the parent's context grows by that one summary, instead of the entire transcript.

34
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
If you remember one thing about sub-agents, make it this. Their value isn't just parallelism,

35
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
it's keeping junk out of your main window.

36
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
So, why actually reach for a sub-agent? Four reasons, and they stack.

37
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,000
First, context isolation, which we just covered. The intermediate tool output stays out of the parent.

38
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Second, parallelization.

39
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Independent sub-agents run at the same time, so a batch of subtasks finishes in the time of the slowest one,

40
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
not the sum of all of them.

41
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Third, tool restriction. You give each sub-agent only the tools its job needs,

42
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
which shrinks the surface for mistakes and keeps it on task.

43
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Fourth, specialized prompts. Each sub-agent gets its own system prompt and instructions, tuned for

44
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,000
one job it does well.

45
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Isolation, parallelism, restriction, focus, that's the case for delegating.

46
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Let's make delegation concrete. Your main agent, the manager, is working a big goal.

47
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:11,000
It hits a chunk of work that stands on its own, say,

48
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
researching one file while other work continues. It hands that chunk to a sub-agent as a scoped task.

49
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Here's the catch you can't skip, that sub-agent starts completely fresh.

50
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
It doesn't inherit the manager's conversation or history. It sees only the prompt you hand it.

51
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:38,000
So, anything it needs to do, the job, the relevant context, the constraints, you pass in explicitly.

52
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
The sub-agent goes off, does its work in isolation, and returns its final result.

53
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:47,000
The manager takes that answer and folds it into the larger task.

54
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Three ideas lock in how isolation works. First, the manager, sometimes called the orchestrator,

55
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
that's the lead agent that breaks the goal into scoped sub-tasks, hands them out, and stitches the

56
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,000
answers back together.

57
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Second, no shared context, and you know that rule from the isolation lesson, so here's where it bites in practice.

58
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Picture two parallel workers researching different parts of a codebase.

59
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Neither one sees what the other found,

60
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
so if worker two needs worker one's result, the manager has to carry it across in the next handoff.

61
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
State never leaks between sub-agents on its own. Third, bounded token growth.

62
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Because each sub-agent returns only a summary, the parent's context grows slowly,

63
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
even when you fire off many sub-tasks. That bound is exactly what lets an agent scale to big,

64
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:44,000
multi-step jobs without its window ballooning.

65
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
So, how do you pick?

66
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Think of it as matching the pattern to the problem. When the model needs to act, look at what

67
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
happened and act again.

68
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
That's the tool use loop doing its job. When a piece of work would generate a mountain of intermediate tokens

69
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
you don't want in your main window,

70
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
that's your cue to hand it to a sub-agent. When you've got several independent sub-tasks, run them

71
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
as parallel sub-agents and

72
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
collapse the wall clock time. And

73
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,000
underneath all of it, keep each agent's tools scoped to just what its job requires. The best agents

74
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
don't pick one pattern,

75
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
they layer all of them.

76
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Let's recap. Two patterns carry every agent. The tool use loop lets the model act, observe, and

77
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
iterate until it answers.

78
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Sub-agents isolate the sub-tasks so only the final message returns, buying you parallelism, tool restriction, and clean

79
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
parental context and

80
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
specialized focus.

81
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Just remember, you layer them, you don't choose between them. And that covers tool use loops and sub-agent patterns.

82
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
I'll see you in the next one.

